        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "EFFECT OF ALLOYED Re ON THE CRITICAL PITTING POTENTIALS OF 18%Cr/10%Ni STAINLESS STEELS"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 1
            },
            "bbox": [
                217,
                330,
                754,
                368
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "H. BöHNI and H. H. UHLIG"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                387,
                390,
                584,
                405
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Department of Metallurgy and Materials Science, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, Massachusetts"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                218,
                407,
                753,
                434
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Abstract—Stainless steels of the 18–8 variety at 25°C in 0·1N NaCl or 0·1N NaBr show increasingly noble critical pitting potentials, but at 0°C decreasingly noble critical potentials with added per cent Re. A likely cause is temperature-sensitive hydration effects within the double layer."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                191,
                454,
                781,
                491
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "IN A RECENT paper¹ it was shown that Mo alloyed with "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "1 5 \\% \\mathrm { { C r } / 1 3 \\% N i }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "stainless steel shifted the critical pitting potential in 0·1N NaCl in the noble direction corresponding to the well-known improved resistance of such alloys to pitting corrosion. The noble shift of potential, however, was shown to occur only at room and at somewhat above room temperature "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "( 4 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathbf { C } ) . \\ \\mathbf { A } \\mathbf { t } \\ 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathbf { C } ,"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "by way of contrast, the effect of Mo was to lower the critical pitting potential or to shift it in the active direction corresponding to decreased resistance to pitting corrosion. Immersion tests in "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "1 0 \\% \\mathrm { F e C l _ { 3 } }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "confirmed that whereas pitting of "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\textbf { a } 2 { \\cdot } 4 \\% \\mathbf { M } \\mathrm { o }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "stainless steel did not occur within two days at "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "2 5 \\mathrm { ^ \\circ C } ,"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "pitting was observed under the same conditions at "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathfrak { o } \\mathfrak { c } ."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "This is opposite to the effect found for 18-8 stainless steel without Mo, pitting being observed at "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\displaystyle 2 5 \\mathrm { ‰}"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "but not at "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\boldsymbol { 0 ^ { \\circ } } \\mathbf { C }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ". It was of interest to learn whether alloyed "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { R e } ,"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "which is also reported to improve pitting resistance of 18–8 stainless steel,² shows a similar temperature effect."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                191,
                508,
                782,
                693
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Alloys were prepared by vacuum melting in dense alumina crucibles decarburized electrolytic "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { F e } ,"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "electrolytic Cr and carbonyl "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { N i } ,"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "the latter supplied by courtesy of the International Nickel Company. Re containing 15ppm Fe but less than 1ppm of any other detectable impurity was supplied by courtesy of Ledgemont Laboratory, Kennecott Copper Corporation. The melt was cast in a purified argon atmosphere by drawing into 7mm dia. Vycor tubes and quenching in water. The alloys were homogenized at "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "1 0 5 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "for 17h, swaged to 0·4cm dia. rods, annealed at "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "1 0 5 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "and water quenched. They were mostly austenitic as quenched, being only slightly magnetic. Chemical analyses are given in Table 1. Electrodes measuring about "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "2 \\mathsf { c m } \\times 0 { \\cdot } 4 \\mathsf { c m }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "dia. were abraded to "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "3 / 0"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "emery paper and then pickled in "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "1 5 \\% \\mathrm { { H N O _ { 3 } } , 2 \\% H F \\ a t \\ 8 0 ^ { \\circ } C }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Mounting of the alloy specimens using a Teflon gasket to ensure exposure only of the alloy to the electrolyte was the same as previously described.¹ Potentiostatic polarization curves were obtained at "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\textstyle 2 5 ^ { \\circ } \\mathbf { C }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "and at "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\textstyle { \\boldsymbol { 0 } } ^ { \\circ } \\mathbf { C }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "in "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { N _ { 2 } } ."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "-saturated 0·1N NaCl and 0·1N NaBr. The electrode totally immersed was first polarized in the least noble passive region for 15min, then the potential was moved in the noble direction 50mV every 5min until the pitting potential was approximated and the current showed a marked increase. This was followed by longer-time runs of 12-15h at a single potential in the neighbourhood of the critical potential in order to establish the steady state value. The least noble potential at which the current did not continuously increase with time and at which no pits could be observed under a low power microscope was taken as the final value. Reproducibility was "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\pm 1 0 \\mathrm { - } \\pm 1 5 \\mathrm { m V }"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                191,
                693,
                782,
                862
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_header",
            "content": {
                "page_header_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Corrosion Science, 1969, Vol. 9, pp. 353 to 355. Pergamon Press. Printed in Great Britain"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
